#Memory
	* sudo swapoff -a 
	* sudo swapon -a
	* free -h
		---Swap memory in Linux acts as a backup to physical RAM
		---When RAM fills up, the Linux kernel moves idle or infrequently used data to swap space, freeing up RAM for active applications
		---It functions as a safety net to prevent system crashes
	* sudo visudo /etc/fstab 
		-comment out the line with swap on it to permanetly disable it
			---The /etc/fstab (file system table) file is a critical system configuration file in Linux that defines how and where storage drives, disk partitions, and remote network shares are automatically mounted during the boot process
